  "facts": {
    "C121": {
      "text": "La Niece and Martin reported that the compounds forming the black patina had not been identified, and a V&A record calls the exact chemical process still unidentified.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "source": "S05",
          "locator": "line 29",
          "quote": "The compound or compounds forming the patina have not been identified."
        },
        {
          "source": "S10",
          "locator": "line 5",
          "quote": "The exact chemical process which takes place is still unidentified."
        }
      ]
    },
    "C124": {
      "text": "An IIT Madras thesis inferred that the copper-enriched surface converts in nitrate solution to CuO and Cu2O, and that these oxides give the black appearance.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "source": "S07",
          "locator": "line 828",
          "quote": "From the current investigation, the following can be inferred"
        },
        {
          "source": "S07",
          "locator": "lines 834-836",
          "quote": "In such a solution zinc dissolves preferentially leading to copper enriched surface, which in nitrate containing solution converts to CuO and Cu2O. These oxides render the black appearance."
        }
      ]
    },
    "C125": {
      "text": "X-ray diffraction of bidri patinas found simonkolleite, zincite and cuprite as the main crystalline phases, which are white or reddish rather than black.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "source": "S07",
          "locator": "lines 359-363",
          "quote": "identified the three main crystalline components of all the object and experimental patinas analysed:- Simonkolleite, zinc hydroxychloride (Zn5(OH)8 Cl2) Zincite, zinc oxide (ZnO) Cuprite, copper I oxide (Cu2O)"
        },
        {
          "source": "S07",
          "locator": "lines 364-365",
          "quote": "The two zinc compounds identified are white in colour and cuprite appears reddish, so these results do not directly explain the black colouration of the patina."
        }
      ]
    },
    "C131": {
      "text": "An IIT Madras thesis found that 20 g of ammonium chloride and 5 g of potassium nitrate in 100 ml of water gave a black patina like that from Bidri sand.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "source": "S07",
          "locator": "lines 668-670",
          "quote": "5g of potassium nitrate (KNO3), 20g of ammonium chloride (NH4Cl) was dissolved in 100 ml of de-ionised water"
        },
        {
          "source": "S07",
          "locator": "lines 829-831",
          "quote": "An alternative to Bidri sand has been identified. A mixture of NH4Cl and KNO3 (20g:5g) in 100mL of de-ionized water renders black patina on the surface similar to one seen with Bidri sand."
        }
      ]
    }
  },
